The Law of Demeter
================

The Law of Demeter is a principle in software design that aims to minimize the coupling between objects in a system. It was first introduced by Bertrand Meyer in 1994. The law states that an object should only depend on its immediate neighbors, and not on any other objects that are not its direct neighbors.

In other words, an object should not have a reference to another object that is not its direct neighbor, unless there is a clear and direct relationship between them. This principle helps to reduce the complexity of a system, make it easier to understand and maintain, and improve its scalability.

Violation of the Law of Demeter
-----------------------------

Here is an example of a Java class that violates the Law of Demeter:
```java
public class Employee {
    private String name;
    private String department;
    private List<String> manager;

    public Employee(String name, String department) {
        this.name = name;
        this.department = department;
    }

    public void setManager(String manager) {
        this.manager = manager;
    }

    public void printDetails() {
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
        System.out.println("Department: " + department);
        if (manager != null) {
            System.out.println("Manager: " + manager);
        }
    }
}

public class Department {
    private String name;
    private List<Employee> employees;

    public Department(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void addEmployee(Employee employee) {
        employees.add(employee);
    }

    public void printEmployees() {
        for (Employee employee : employees) {
            employee.printDetails();
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, the `Employee` class has a reference to the `Department` class through the `manager` field, which is not its direct neighbor. The `Department` class also has a reference to the `Employee` class through the `employees` field, which is also not its direct neighbor.

This violates the Law of Demeter because the `Employee` class is depending on the `Department` class, which is not its direct neighbor. This creates a tight coupling between the two classes, making it harder to understand and maintain the system.

