The Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) is a design principle in object-oriented programming that states:

"Instead of making many clients depend on an interface, make each client depend on the interface that it uses."

In other words, instead of having a single, very broad interface that many classes implement, it's better to have multiple, more specialized interfaces that each class implements, depending on its specific needs.

Here's an example in Java:

**Before (Bad Design)**

```java
public interface PaymentGateway {
    void processPayment();
    void refundPayment();
    void sendInvoice();
    void sendConfirmationEmail();
}

public class PayPal implements PaymentGateway {
    // implementation
}

public class Stripe implements PaymentGateway {
    // implementation
}

public class BankTransfer implements PaymentGateway {
    // implementation
}
```

In this example, the `PaymentGateway` interface has many methods that are not relevant to all payment gateways. The `PayPal` class, `Stripe` class, and `BankTransfer` class all implement the same interface, which means they all have to implement these unnecessary methods.

**After (Good Design)**

```java
public interface PayPalGateway {
    void processPayment();
    void sendConfirmationEmail();
}

public interface StripeGateway {
    void processPayment();
    void sendInvoice();
}

public interface BankTransferGateway {
    void processPayment();
    void refundPayment();
}

public class PayPal implements PayPalGateway {
    // implementation
}

public class Stripe implements StripeGateway {
    // implementation
}

public class BankTransfer implements BankTransferGateway {
    // implementation
}
```

In this example, each payment gateway interface has only the methods that are relevant to that specific gateway. The `PayPal` class, `Stripe` class, and `BankTransfer` class all implement the interfaces that are relevant to their specific needs.

By using multiple, more specialized interfaces, we've made each class more focused and easier to maintain. We've also reduced the coupling between classes, making it easier to change or replace one class without affecting others.

Note that this is just one possible way to apply the ISP principle. The key idea is to identify the specific interfaces that each class needs to implement, and to create those interfaces, rather than relying on a single, broad interface.

